      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "AIM",
          "content": "Coronary collateral circulation (CCC) has an important impact on cardiovascular prognosis and well-developed CCC is associated with better clinical outcomes. We investigated whether lymphocyte/monocyte ratio (LMR) has an association with CCC in patients with stable coronary artery disease (SCAD)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "The study population consisted of 245 patients with SCAD. Patients were classified into a poor CCC group (Rentrop grades 0/1, n = 87), or good CCC group (Rentrop grades 2/3, n = 158)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "LMR values were significantly higher in patients with good CCC than in those with poor CCC (4.41 ± 1.58 vs 2.76 ± 1.10; p < 0.001). In receiver operating characteristic analysis, optimal cutoff of LMR for predicting well-developed CCC was 3.38. In multivariate analysis, LMR >3.38 (OR 4.637; p = 0.004), high sensitivity C-reactive protein (OR 0.810, p < 0.001), dyslipidemia (OR 2.485; p = 0.039), and presence of chronic total occlusion (OR 16.836; p < 0.001) were independent predictors of well-developed CCC."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Increased LMR predicts well-developed CCC in SCAD patients."
        }
      ]},
